-- title:
--   Lemma 4.7: the feedback controls have finite expected cost, $\mathbb E[\sum_tC_t(\hat a_t)]<\infty$
-- statement:
--   Under the §4 standing assumptions, let $J$ satisfy H Hypothesis, let $V_t$ be defined by (4.11) from $J$ with optimizers $a_t$, and let $\hat a$ be the feedback controls (4.18). Then
--   $$\mathbb E\Big[\sum_{t=0}^{N-1}C_t(\hat a_t)\Big]<\infty.$$
--
--   With Assumption 4.1(v) this bounds $\mathbb E[G(\sum_t\hat a_t)]$, which the proof of Theorem 4.1 needs to keep the fixed-point interval bounded.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The expectation of the nonnegative cost is a $[0,\infty]$-valued integral.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: J^n, V^n_t, a^n_t, â^n → a single J, V_t, a_t, â; reason: the superscript n is vestigial: the lemma concerns one J.
--   - encoding: E[Σ_t C_t(â_t)] < ∞ → lintegral of the nonnegative cost < ⊤; reason: a Bochner integral "< ∞" would be vacuous.
--   - encoding: V_t from (4.10)–(4.11) → valueENN: the supremum over a ≥ 0 of the kernel conditional expectation of V_{t+1}(x + a) minus C_t(a), in [0, ∞]; value is its real part; reason: the supremum in [0, ∞] has no junk value; conditional expectations as in the kernel encoding.
--   - encoding: a_t(x) the optimizer for V_t(x) → any functions a_t(x, y) ≥ 0 attaining the supremum in (4.11) with a finite objective, for a.e. y, every t < N and x ≥ 0 (IsOptimizer); reason: the optimizer is unique (C_t strictly convex), so this covers the paper's optimizer without choosing it.
--   - encoding: periods k = 0, …, N − 1 with noises X_{k+1}, Y_{k+1}; F^Y_k = σ(Y_1, …, Y_k) → Lean coordinates X k, Y k (0-based); F^Y_k is generated by the k-prefix of the noise path (truncPath); reason: Lean convention; same instances.
--   - generalization: the standing model of §3/§4 also fixes the idiosyncratic noise X (independent of Y, X_1, …, X_N independent) and φ_k with (3.1) → no assumption on X or φ; reason: X and φ do not occur in the statement, which therefore specializes to the standing model.
--   - encoding: (3.6) of H Hypothesis, an inequality between random variables 'for all x ≥ 0, k' → almost surely, simultaneously for all x ≥ 0 and k (Bound36); reason: x ↦ J(x, y) is continuous on ℝ₊ (H Hypothesis (1)) and the costs C_k are C¹, so 'for each x, a.s.' and 'a.s., for all x' are the same condition; same instances.
-- source:
--   Zhou, Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution games, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), Lemma 4.7, p.24

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_Setup
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_HHypothesis
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_ValueRecursion

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

namespace RelPerfMFG

/-- **Lemma 4.7**, Zhou, *Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution
games*, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), p.24. Let `J : ℝ₊ × (ℝᵉ)ᴺ → ℝ₊` satisfy H Hypothesis.
Define `V_t` via (4.11) w.r.t. `J` and let `a_t` be its optimizer. Define `â` by (4.18). Then
`E[Σ_{t=0}^{N−1} C_t(â_t)] < ∞`.

**Formalization Note.** The printed superscript `n` is vestigial (a single `J`). §4 standing
assumptions on `μ`, `Y`, `C_t`. Optimizers as in `IsOptimizer`, `â` the feedback recursion
(4.18) evaluated at `Y`. The expectation of the nonnegative cost is a `[0, ∞]`-valued integral,
so "`< ∞`" is not vacuous. 0-based periods. -/
theorem lemma_4_7 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {N e : ℕ}
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (Y : Ω → NoisePath N e) (hY : Measurable Y)
    (C : Fin N → ℝ → ℝ) (hC : IsCostS4 C)
    (J : ℝ → NoisePath N e → ℝ) (hJ : HHypothesis μ Y C J)
    (aOpt : ℕ → ℝ → NoisePath N e → ℝ) (ha : IsOptimizer μ Y C J aOpt) :
    ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (∑ t : Fin N, C t (feedback aOpt t (Y ω))) ∂μ < ⊤ := by sorry

end RelPerfMFG
